---

<ResearchLayout
  slug="drug-development"
  number="03"
  topic="Drug development"
  title="Drug development."
  accent="From targets to candidates."
  description="We combine AI-enabled computer-aided drug design with laboratory experiments to discover and refine small molecules that target mechanisms of resistant prostate cancer."
  question="Can a molecular mechanism that helps cancer survive become a starting point for a new therapy?"
  keywords={['Computer-aided drug design', 'TOP2 and LIN28', 'Preclinical research']}
  sections={[
    { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
    { id: 'targets', label: 'Research programmes' },
    { id: 'discovery-process', label: 'Our discovery process' },
    { id: 'translation', label: 'Towards translation' },
  ]}
  publications={publications}
>
  <!-- Edit the text below to update this page. Use <strong>text</strong> for bold. -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">TURNING BIOLOGY INTO OPPORTUNITY</p>
    <h2 id="overview-heading">Drug discovery begins with a biological question.</h2>
    <p>
      Understanding a mechanism of treatment resistance can reveal a new molecular
      target. We investigate whether small molecules can disrupt those mechanisms
      and create opportunities to treat prostate cancers that remain dependent on
      the androgen receptor, as well as those that have become less dependent on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our approach connects <strong>AI-enabled computer-aided drug design</strong> with
      biochemical assays and disease models. Computational work helps prioritize
      compounds; experiments determine what those compounds actually do and guide
      the next round of design.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="rd-section" id="targets" aria-labelledby="targets-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH PROGRAMMES</p>
    <h2 id="targets-heading">Two routes into cancer cell biology.</h2>
    <div class="rd-focus-list">
      <article class="rd-focus">
        <span class="rd-focus-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>TOP2: targeting a dependency of proliferating cells</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Topoisomerase II (TOP2)</strong> helps manage DNA topology during
            essential cellular processes. Our work has developed catalytic inhibitors
            designed to interfere with TOP2 function. Published studies describe
            compounds that block TOP2–DNA interaction and examine how chemical
            modifications affect activity, solubility and stability.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="rd-focus">
        <span class="rd-focus-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>LIN28: targeting RNA regulation and stem-like programmes</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>LIN28</strong> is an RNA-binding protein involved in gene networks
            associated with cancer stemness and treatment resistance. We investigate
            small molecules that interrupt its interaction with RNA. Our published
            work combines computational design with biochemical and cellular studies
            to assess these compounds and their effects on cancer cell growth.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="rd-section" id="discovery-process" aria-labelledby="process-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">OUR DISCOVERY PROCESS</p>
    <h2 id="process-heading">Design. Test. Learn. Refine.</h2>
    <p>
      Drug discovery is an iterative process. Each stage provides evidence that helps
      us decide which compounds to investigate further and which properties need improvement.
    </p>
    <ol class="rd-process">
      <li>
        <h3>Understand the target</h3>
        <p>Investigate a protein's role in resistant disease and identify the activity or molecular interaction that a compound should disrupt.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Prioritize and design compounds</h3>
        <p>Use computational screening, structural modelling and AI-enabled approaches to identify chemical starting points and guide modifications.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Test the mechanism and cellular effects</h3>
        <p>Use biochemical assays and cancer cell models to examine target activity, molecular interactions and the effects of compounds on growth.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Refine promising candidates</h3>
        <p>Bring experimental results back into compound design, and use appropriate preclinical models to investigate activity and properties relevant to further development.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="rd-section" id="translation" aria-labelledby="translation-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">TOWARDS TRANSLATION</p>
    <h2 id="translation-heading">Building evidence for the next step.</h2>
    <p>
      The lab's drug-discovery work has generated patented compound series targeting
      TOP2 and LIN28. Our goal is to build on these chemical starting points to develop
      new therapeutic strategies and investigate opportunities to improve existing treatments.
    </p>
    <div class="rd-callout">
      <h3>Preclinical research</h3>
      <p>
        These programmes study experimental compounds. Activity in biochemical assays,
        cells or animal models is an important research step, but does not establish
        safety or effectiveness in patients. Further evaluation is needed as candidates
        progress towards potential clinical development.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ResearchLayout>
